{
  // Solution-style config: this file type-checks nothing itself (`files: []`)
  // and instead points `tsc --build` at `tsconfig.src.json`, the action's own
  // config. `docs/tsconfig.json` — the site's self-managing config with its
  // own DOM/React types — is deliberately NOT referenced here. It used to
  // be: CI's Lint job runs `hk check --all` -> `bun run typecheck` ->
  // `tsc --build`, but `.github/actions/setup/action.yml` installs only the
  // repository root (`bun install --frozen-lockfile`), never
  // `docs/node_modules`. With the reference in place, that job hit 40+
  // `TS2307 Cannot find module '@docusaurus/…'` on every run — invisible on
  // any machine that had ever run `mise run docs:*` and gained
  // `docs/node_modules` locally, which is how the defect reached review
  // undetected. The site is instead type-checked by `mise run
  // docs:typecheck`, which `gh-pages.yml` runs (with `dir = "docs"`, so it
  // installs and uses the site's own `tsconfig.json`) before `mise run
  // docs:build`. Installing `docs/` in the shared setup action was rejected
  // as the fix: it would cost every action CI job the whole Docusaurus tree
  // — 320 packages/21.9s for the action alone versus 644/61.3s with `docs/`
  // pulled in, the same cost CLAUDE.md's Bun-workspace bullet measured and
  // rejected for the identical reason. See CLAUDE.md → "`docs/` is a
  // Docusaurus site" for the full writeup.
  //
  // `compilerOptions.paths` below is a deliberate duplicate of
  // `tsconfig.src.json`'s, not a stray leftover: it is here for a consumer
  // other than `tsc`. Bun resolves the `@/*`/`@rust-toolchain*` aliases the
  // test suite imports through by reading `compilerOptions.paths` off the
  // nearest tsconfig.json to the repo root, and it does not follow
  // `references` to find one — without this, `bun test` fails every test
  // file with "Cannot find module '@/...'". `extends` was tried first and
  // rejected: it also pulls in `tsconfig.src.json`'s `include: ["**/*.ts"]`,
  // which — unlike `files`, `include` is additive, not replaced by an empty
  // `files: []` — put every source file back into this program directly and
  // broke `tsc --build` with TS6305/TS6306 against the referenced project.
  // Repeating just `paths` here has no such effect, since `files: []` with
  // no `include` still selects zero inputs for `tsc` itself.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"],
      "@rust-toolchain": ["./src/lib.ts"],
      "@rust-toolchain/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  "files": [],
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.src.json" }]
}
